<Guide lang="en" title="For people with no religion" description="An honest introduction to Islam for people who keep religion at a distance: reason, science, meaning, and why Muslims believe.">
  <p>More than half of Korean adults have no religion. Many feel they live perfectly well without one, and are tired of people who evangelise hard. We respect that. This page is not trying to persuade you to believe anything. It is an honest account of why Muslims believe.</p>

  <blockquote><p>"There is no compulsion in religion." (Qur'an 2:256)</p></blockquote>

  <h2>Why do Muslims believe?</h2>
  <p>Everyone's reasons differ, but these are things Korean Muslims often say.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"The world seems too finely made to be an accident."</strong> The Qur'an calls the heavens and the earth, the alternation of night and day, and the order of nature "signs for people who think", and repeatedly tells its readers to reflect (3:190). Faith is not treated as the opposite of reason.</li>
    <li><strong>"Nobody stands between me and God."</strong> Islam has no priestly class or intermediary. Anyone can pray to God and ask forgiveness directly, anytime and anywhere.</li>
    <li><strong>"My day has a rhythm."</strong> Many say the five daily prayers put a pause into days worn down by competition and comparison.</li>
    <li><strong>"My life has a direction."</strong> Having a measure bigger than money, grades or other people's opinions feels, to many, like freedom.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Doesn't it clash with science?</h2>
  <p>The medieval Islamic world was a centre of mathematics, astronomy and medicine. The words "algebra" and "algorithm" come from the Muslim scholar al-Khwarizmi. The very first word of the Qur'an to be revealed was "Read!" (96:1), which shows how much learning matters to Muslims. Many Muslim scientists and doctors hold faith and science together.</p>

  <h2>Doesn't religion end up causing conflict?</h2>
  <p>It is true that conflict and violence have happened in the name of religion, including in the name of Islam, and Muslims feel that painfully. But such things usually came from power and politics more than from religious teaching, and within the same religion there were people who opposed them first. <a href="/en/questions/violence/">Is Islam a religion of violence?</a></p>

  <h2>It's fine not to believe</h2>
  <p>The aim of this site is understanding, not conversion. If you get to know Islam and still choose to live without religion, we respect that. If you come away knowing what real Muslims are like, rather than the image from the news, that alone means a great deal.</p>

